<DocsLayout
  title="Desktop UI"
  subtitle="SolidJS in the webview. Projects, CAD, CAM, Library, and Settings work end to end. The long tail is still stubs."
  description="CADCAMFUN desktop UI: routes, feature folders, the Tauri shell, and which screens are real."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP6 on the task board is <strong>in progress</strong>. The board says the Solid and
      Tailwind v3 foundation is in place, with core screens plus plugins, costs, organizations,
      waitlist, auth, and analytics, and that the rest of the legacy pages are still stubs.
      Spec 06 is more specific about what is proven: the Projects, CAD, CAM, Library, and
      Settings screens work end to end against the contract. The 3D viewport plays back
      toolpaths. The 2D editor drives the tool state machines. Owner paths:
      <code>apps/desktop/src/app</code>, <code>features/*/screens</code>,
      <code>shared/components</code>, <code>shared/state</code>, and <code>src/i18n</code>.
      Spec: <code>specs/06-desktop-ui.md</code>.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Do not treat a registered route as a finished screen">
      <p>
        <code>apps/desktop/src/app/routes.tsx</code> lists the legacy URL table, including
        marketplace, chat, billing, and MCP. <code>src/pages</code> holds one adapter per path.
        Many of those adapters still render a stub. Spec 06 says the long tail of legacy pages
        are generated stubs rather than implemented screens.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="boot" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boot</h2>
    <p>
      The webview asks the shell for <code>get_sidecar_endpoint</code>, receives
      <code>&#123; baseUrl, token &#125;</code>, builds <code>ApiClient.layer</code>, and calls
      <code>GET /health</code>. Effects run on one app <code>ManagedRuntime</code>. Server data
      stays in the API, with a lightweight query cache keyed by endpoint and params. The editor
      undo stack is client state: a command pattern over drawing patches, capped at 200 steps
      (<code>shared/state/history.ts</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      The splash shows Retry and View logs only after the sidecar has stayed unreachable for
      150 seconds. Until then it keeps resolving the endpoint. The shell waits up to 60 seconds
      for each start. The splash shows the seconds waited and a readable reason.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Browser with the fake API, or the Tauri window">
{`bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop dev
bun run desktop:dev`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The fake client is <code>apps/desktop/src/testing/fake-api.ts</code>. Spec 06’s change log
      says that fake still answers 501 for groups added after the first core screens, so plugins,
      costs, organizations, auth, and analytics screens need a live sidecar. There is no DOM
      component test for those screens yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="routes" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Routes</h2>
    <p>
      Paths below exist in <code>routes.tsx</code>. Spec 06 names Projects, CAD, CAM, Library,
      and Settings as the working end-to-end screens. The activity bar also links Costs, AI,
      and Plugins. Materials, tools, machine, and toolpaths are in the route table. Treat
      anything past that working set as a stub until you open the screen module and see a real
      feature page.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Path</th><th>What to expect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>/projects</code>, <code>/projects/:id</code>, <code>/projects/create</code></td><td>Working project list and open flow (spec 06).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/projects/:id/history</code></td><td>Version-control route. Spec 13 says the screen and per-item History buttons are built. A commit graph and in-editor history panels are not.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/cad</code></td><td>Working CAD editor. Page file re-exports <code>features/cad</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/cam</code></td><td>Working CAM workspace: operations, generator, G-code, verify tab, toolpath preview.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/library</code></td><td>Working library screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/settings</code>, <code>/settings/companion</code></td><td>Working settings. Companion pairing UI lives on the companion route.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/materials</code>, <code>/tools</code>, <code>/machine</code></td><td>Registered. Detail routes exist (<code>/materials/:id</code> and the same shape for tools and machine).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/toolpaths</code>, <code>/toolpaths/:id</code></td><td>Registered. Spec 06 notes both still mount the CAM workspace screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/costs</code></td><td>Registered. Estimate screen exists and needs a live sidecar for the groups the fake rejects.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/ai</code></td><td>Registered assistant page. Docked chat also exists inside CAD and CAM.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/plugins</code>, <code>/plugins/:id</code></td><td>Registered. List, enable, and zip install go through the legacy plugin HTTP group.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/auth/*</code>, <code>/organizations</code>, <code>/analytics</code>, <code>/waitlist</code></td><td>Screens added in the integration pass. Still part of the unfinished parity tail relative to the full legacy app.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/marketplace</code>, <code>/mcp</code>, chat under organizations</td><td>Routes exist. Spec 06 calls the long tail stubs.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Layout: title bar (native menus, in-window fallback on Windows and Linux), left activity
      bar, main area, bottom status bar (units, snap, cursor, sidecar status). Command palette
      is <code>Cmd/Ctrl+K</code>. Theme is light, dark, or system through CSS variables. Locale
      file for v6.0 is <code>src/i18n/en.json</code> plus feature files such as
      <code>en.cad.json</code>. Interactive controls are keyboard reachable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="folders" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Folders</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Path</th><th>Put this here</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>src/app/</code></td><td><code>App.tsx</code>, <code>Shell.tsx</code>, <code>routes.tsx</code>, <code>client.ts</code>, <code>sidecar.ts</code>, <code>queries.ts</code>. Nothing feature-specific.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/pages/</code></td><td>One-line adapters. A page re-exports a screen. No product logic. <code>PageStub</code> is the placeholder.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/features/&lt;name&gt;/</code></td><td><code>screens/</code>, <code>components/</code>, <code>hooks/</code>, <code>lib/</code>, <code>store/</code>, <code>types/</code>. Present areas: admin, ai, analytics, auth, billing, cad, cam, chat, companion, costs, dashboard, legal, library, marketplace, mcp, organizations, plugins, profile, projects, settings, toolpaths.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/shared/ui/</code></td><td>Button, modal, card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/shared/components/</code></td><td>Title bar, status bar, workspace navigation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/shared/state/</code></td><td>Session, settings, query cache, editor commands and history.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/styles/</code></td><td>Global stylesheets. Feature CSS stays in <code>features/&lt;name&gt;/styles/</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/viewport/</code></td><td>WP7. See <a href="/docs/viewport">Viewport</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/i18n/</code></td><td>Translation tables.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/testing/</code></td><td>Fake API and core-flow tests.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      New logic goes in a feature folder, or in <code>shared/</code> when two features use it.
      It does not go in <code>pages/</code>. Forms that mirror CAM parameters should derive from
      the Effect schema (<code>shared/components/schema-form.ts</code>) so the UI cannot drift
      from <code>packages/core</code>. <code>Schema.optional(X)</code> is a union of <code>X</code>
      and <code>undefined</code>. The form description unwraps that.
    </p>
    <p>
      CAD and CAM share a browser (<code>features/cad/lib/browser.ts</code> and
      <code>features/cad/components/browser/</code>). Document units and tolerance change in one
      place, <code>state/document-settings.ts</code>. Request bodies go through
      <code>state/json-safe.ts</code> so <code>undefined</code>, NaN, and typed arrays do not
      break JSON encoding. Session tokens live in <code>shared/state/session.ts</code> (a ref
      mirrored to <code>localStorage</code>). Sign-out drops the query cache.
    </p>
    <p>
      CAM Save opens one dialog from the toolbar and from the generator. It can export G-code
      and a portable JSON toolpath. Reopening a saved program uses the toolpath version API.
      Editing NC drops obsolete kernel and verification evidence. Undo and redo for CAM are a
      session snapshot (NC, IR, settings, stock), separate from database versions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="shell" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Shell</h2>
    <p>
      WP5 owns <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri</code>. The task board marks M1 done: clippy and 32
      Rust tests, verified with a stub sidecar. Spec 07 §7 records menus, tray, secrets, and
      lifecycle code beyond that milestone, and a later run of 60 tests with 2 ignored (OS
      keychain and the real-binary parent contract). A signed <code>.app</code> is WP10 and is
      not verified.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>File</th><th>Role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>src/sidecar.rs</code></td><td>Spawn, token, ready-line decoder, restart once, stdin shutdown.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/commands.rs</code></td><td><code>get_sidecar_endpoint</code>, <code>get_logs_dir</code>, <code>open_path</code>, <code>pick_file</code>, <code>save_file</code>, secret get/set/delete.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/secrets.rs</code></td><td>OS keychain, service <code>com.cadcamfun.app</code>. <code>keyring</code> 3.6.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/menu.rs</code>, <code>src/tray.rs</code></td><td>Menu ids such as <code>file.new</code>, <code>view.preset_top</code>. Tray recent projects emit <code>tray.recent.&lt;projectId&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>src/updater.rs</code></td><td>WP10 updater plugin. Packaged verification is outstanding.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Commands the webview can call are listed in spec 07 §1. New commands need an
      <code>allow-&lt;name&gt;</code> entry in <code>capabilities/main.json</code>. The CSP has
      no <code>unsafe-eval</code> and no remote hosts in the production policy.
      <code>open_path</code> refuses executables and only opens files under app data, logs,
      documents, downloads, and desktop. File associations mentioned in the spec are
      <code>.cadcam</code>, <code>.nc</code>, and <code>.gcode</code>. The single-instance plugin
      focuses the existing window.
    </p>
    <p>
      macOS glass uses <code>tauri-plugin-liquid-glass</code> and a transparent window. The UI
      sets <code>data-glass</code> from <code>app/glass.ts</code> after the plugin accepts the
      effect. An App Store build turns private API off via <code>tauri.appstore.conf.json</code>.
      Direct distribution is the current release channel.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The webview must not import Drizzle or open SQLite. Data access goes through <code>ApiClient</code>.</li>
      <li><code>src/pages</code> must not grow logic. Put it in <code>features/</code> or <code>shared/</code>.</li>
      <li>The Rust shell must not contain toolpath math, solid kernels, or repository queries.</li>
      <li>Secrets must not be stored in SQLite or in plain files. The keychain and the sidecar credentials file are the two stores, and they are different keys.</li>
      <li>The per-launch token must not be written to disk or to logs.</li>
      <li>A stub route must not be documented as a working end-to-end screen. Spec 06 names five: Projects, CAD, CAM, Library, Settings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
